+# Nabaztag V2 - JTAG Recovery Guide
+
+## What We're Flashing
+
+**WPA2/3 firmware with ORIGINAL (unmodified) MTL boot pages.**
+
+The build script (`scripts/build-nabgcc.sh --release`) will:
+1. Compile the **original** MTL source (`boot.0.0.0.13.mtl`) into bytecode
+2. Embed that bytecode into `src/bc.c`
+3. Cross-compile the C firmware (with all WPA2/3 fixes applied)
+4. Produce `bin/Nab.bin` (~102KB)
+
+Before building, the MTL file must be **reverted to the original version** (undo
+the HTML5 changes in the working tree). The WPA2/3 C code fixes in `eapol.c` and
+`ieee80211.c` stay as-is.
+
+### WPA2/3 Fixes Applied (C code - these are good)
+
+1. **GMCS in association RSN IE** (`src/net/ieee80211.c`): Extended RSN IE from
+   20 to 26 bytes, adding PMKID Count=0 + Group Management Cipher Suite
+   (BIP-CMAC-128, `00:0F:AC:06`)
+2. **GMCS in EAPOL RSN IE template** (`src/net/eapol.c`): Extended `wpa2_rsn[]`
+   from 22 to 28 bytes with matching GMCS
+3. **EAPOL cipher comparison fix** (`src/net/eapol.c`): Changed to mask comparison
+   `(ieee80211_encryption & 0xF0) == IEEE80211_CRYPT_WPA2` in msg 2/4, 4/4, GTK
+4. **Mirror AP's key_desc_ver** (`src/net/eapol.c`): Added `ap_key_desc_ver`
+   variable, mirror in msg 2/4, 4/4, GTK (fixes WPA3 transition mode where AP
+   sends key_desc_ver=0)
+
+---
+
+## Hardware Setup
+
+### Target Device
+- **MCU**: ML67Q4051 (OKI/Lapis), ARM7TDMI, ~60MHz
+- **Flash**: 128KB IntROM at `0x08000000` (124KB usable, 4KB config sector at `0x1F000`)
+- **RAM**: 16KB IntRAM at `0x10000000`, 1MB ExtRAM at `0xD0000000`
+- **Voltage**: 3.3V (matches RPi GPIO - direct connection, no level shifter needed)
+
+### JTAG Debugger
+- **Raspberry Pi 1 Model B** (BCM2835, 3.3V GPIO, 26-pin P1 header)
+- OpenOCD `bcm2835gpio` bit-bang interface
+
+---
+
+## J29 JTAG Pinout (Confirmed from FCC Schematic)
+
+Source: FCC ID TYR-TAGTAG, "schematics main board" (VIOLET - ESME Sudria, LLC2_4d)
+
+J29 is an 8-pin in-line header on the PCB (marked "8 KL"), located near the MCU.
+Pin 1 is marked with a square pad. All JTAG signals have 10kohm pull-up resistors
+to 3.3V.
+
+```
+J29 (viewed from component side, pin 1 = square pad)
+
+  Pin 1 = 3.3V (VTref)
+  Pin 2 = GND
+  Pin 3 = nTRST    (MCU pin 82)  -- 10k pull-up R43
+  Pin 4 = TDI      (MCU pin 80)  -- 10k pull-up R45
+  Pin 5 = TMS      (MCU pin 79)  -- 10k pull-up R41
+  Pin 6 = TCK      (MCU pin 78)  -- 10k pull-up R39
+  Pin 7 = TDO      (MCU pin 76)  -- 10k pull-up R40
+  Pin 8 = RESETN   (MCU pin 75)  -- nSRST (system reset)
+```
+
+---
+
+## Wiring: RPi P1 Header -> J29
+
+7 wires total (6 signals + 1 ground). Do NOT connect J29 pin 1 (3.3V) to the
+RPi - the rabbit uses its own power supply. Only share ground.
+
+```
+RPi P1 Pin    GPIO    Signal      J29 Pin
+----------    ----    ------      -------
+    25        GND     GND           2
+    26        GPIO7   nTRST         3
+    19        GPIO10  TDI           4
+    22        GPIO25  TMS           5
+    23        GPIO11  TCK           6
+    21        GPIO9   TDO           7
+    24        GPIO8   RESETN        8
+```
+
+### RPi 1 Model B P1 Header Reference (26-pin)
+
+```
+            3V3  (1)  (2)  5V
+   I2C SDA  (3)  (4)  5V
+   I2C SCL  (5)  (6)  GND
+   GPIO4    (7)  (8)  GPIO14 (UART TX)
+        GND (9)  (10) GPIO15 (UART RX)
+   GPIO17  (11)  (12) GPIO18
+   GPIO27  (13)  (14) GND
+   GPIO22  (15)  (16) GPIO23
+       3V3 (17)  (18) GPIO24
+   GPIO10  (19)  (20) GND          <-- TDI
+    GPIO9  (21)  (22) GPIO25       <-- TDO / TMS
+   GPIO11  (23)  (24) GPIO8        <-- TCK / RESETN
+       GND (25)  (26) GPIO7        <-- GND / nTRST
+```
+
+Most of our signals are on the SPI0 cluster (pins 19-26), which is convenient
+for wiring.
+
+---
+
+## Step-by-Step Recovery
+
+### Step 0: Build the firmware (on Mac)
+
+Before touching the RPi, build the firmware on your Mac:
+
+```bash
+# 1. Build with release flag (runs in Docker)
+cd ../..
+scripts/build-nabgcc.sh --release
+
+# 2. Verify the output
+ls -la vendor/nabgcc/bin/Nab.bin
+# Should be ~102-105 KB
+
+# The .sim file is also generated but we don't need it for JTAG.
+# For JTAG we flash the raw .bin directly.
+```
+
+### Step 1: Prepare the Raspberry Pi
+
+The RPi needs Raspbian/Raspberry Pi OS with OpenOCD installed.
+
+```bash
+# On the RPi:
+sudo apt-get update
+sudo apt-get install -y openocd
+
+# Verify OpenOCD has bcm2835gpio support:
+openocd --version
+# Should be 0.10.0 or later
+```
+
+### Step 2: Copy files to the RPi
+
+From your Mac, copy these files to the RPi (via scp, USB stick, SD card, etc.):
+
+```
+vendor/nabgcc/bin/Nab.bin                          -> ~/nabgcc/bin/Nab.bin
+vendor/nabgcc/openocd/nabaztagv2-rpi.cfg           -> ~/nabgcc/openocd/nabaztagv2-rpi.cfg
+vendor/nabgcc/openocd/interface/rpi1-gpio.cfg      -> ~/nabgcc/openocd/interface/rpi1-gpio.cfg
+vendor/nabgcc/openocd/target/ml67q4051.cfg         -> ~/nabgcc/openocd/target/ml67q4051.cfg
+```
+
+Preserve the directory structure so the `source` paths in the config files work.
+
+```bash
+# From Mac (assuming RPi is on the network as "raspberrypi.local"):
+rsync -avR \
+  vendor/nabgcc/bin/Nab.bin \
+  vendor/nabgcc/openocd/nabaztagv2-rpi.cfg \
+  vendor/nabgcc/openocd/interface/rpi1-gpio.cfg \
+  vendor/nabgcc/openocd/target/ml67q4051.cfg \
+  pi@raspberrypi.local:~/nabgcc/
+```
+
+Or simply copy the whole `vendor/nabgcc/` directory.
+
+### Step 3: Wire the RPi to the rabbit
+
+1. **Power off both devices** (unplug the rabbit, shut down the RPi)
+2. Connect 7 DuPont wires (female-to-female or female-to-male depending on
+   what headers are available) between the RPi P1 header and J29:
+
+   | Wire | RPi P1 Pin | -> | J29 Pin | Signal |
+   |------|-----------|-----|---------|--------|
+   | 1    | 25        | -> | 2       | GND    |
+   | 2    | 26        | -> | 3       | nTRST  |
+   | 3    | 19        | -> | 4       | TDI    |
+   | 4    | 22        | -> | 5       | TMS    |
+   | 5    | 23        | -> | 6       | TCK    |
+   | 6    | 21        | -> | 7       | TDO    |
+   | 7    | 24        | -> | 8       | RESETN |
+
+3. **Double-check** every wire before powering on. Wrong connections can damage
+   either board. The most critical: do NOT connect 5V to anything on the rabbit.
+
+### Step 4: Power on
+
+1. Power on the **RPi first** (boot to Linux)
+2. Then plug in the **rabbit's power supply**
+   - It will show random LEDs and reboot-loop. That's expected.
+   - OpenOCD will assert reset to halt it anyway.
+
+### Step 5: Run OpenOCD
+
+```bash
+cd ~/nabgcc
+sudo openocd -f openocd/nabaztagv2-rpi.cfg
+```
+
+Expected output:
+```
+BCM2835 GPIO JTAG/SWD bitbang driver
+adapter speed: 100 kHz
+Info : clock speed 100 kHz
+Info : JTAG tap: ml67q4051.cpu tap/device found: 0x3f0f0f0f
+Info : ml67q4051.cpu: target state: running
+```
+
+If you see `Error: JTAG scan chain interrogation failed`, check wiring.
+If the TAP ID doesn't match, something is wrong with the JTAG connection.
+
+Leave OpenOCD running. Open a **second terminal** on the RPi.
+
+### Step 6: Flash the firmware
+
+In the second terminal, connect via telnet to OpenOCD's command port:
+
+```bash
+telnet localhost 4444
+```
+
+Then run these commands one at a time:
+
+```
+# 1. Halt the CPU
+reset halt
+
+# 2. Verify we can read flash (optional sanity check)
+flash info 0
+
+# 3. (Optional) Dump current (bricked) flash for backup
+dump_image bricked_backup.bin 0x08000000 0x20000
+
+# 4. Erase and write the new firmware
+flash write_image erase bin/Nab.bin 0x08000000
+
+# 5. Verify the write
+verify_image bin/Nab.bin 0x08000000
+
+# 6. Boot the new firmware
+reset run
+```
+
+Expected output for step 4:
+```
+auto erase enabled
+wrote 102400 bytes from file bin/Nab.bin in Xs (Y KB/s)
+```
+
+Expected output for step 5:
+```
+verified XXXXX bytes in Xs (Y KB/s)
+```
+
+### Step 7: Verify
+
+After `reset run`, the rabbit should:
+1. Show normal LED boot sequence (not random colors)
+2. Enter config mode if you hold the button during power-on
+3. Create a `NabaztagXX` WiFi AP in config mode
+
+If it still shows random LEDs, the bytecode is still bad. See troubleshooting.
+
+### Step 8: Disconnect
+
+1. Shut down OpenOCD (`shutdown` command or Ctrl+C)
+2. Power off the rabbit
+3. Disconnect the wires
+4. Power on the rabbit standalone to verify it boots normally
+
+---
+
+## Alternative: Flash the Original Nab0013 Firmware
+
+If the WPA2/3 build doesn't work, you can extract and flash the original firmware
+from `Nab0013.01ca89.sim`:
+
+```bash
+# Build the sim-to-bin extraction tool (one-time)
+cd tools/mkfirmware
+# (We'd need to add a --decode flag, or use a quick script)
+
+# Or extract manually with Python:
+python3 -c "
+import sys
+data = open('vendor/nabgcc-latest/Nab0013.01ca89.sim', 'rb').read()
+# Strip -violet- markers and size field
+inner = data[16:-8]  # skip '-violet-' + 8-char hex size, strip trailing '-violet-'
+# Hex decode
+raw = bytes.fromhex(inner.decode('ascii'))
+# Decrypt (reverse of strcrypt8 with key=0x47, alpha=47)
+INV8 = [1,171,205,183,57,163,197,239,241,27,61,167,41,19,53,223,225,139,173,151,25,131,165,207,209,251,29,135,9,243,21,191,193,107,141,119,249,99,133,175,177,219,253,103,233,211,245,159,161,75,109,87,217,67,101,143,145,187,221,71,201,179,213,127,129,43,77,55,185,35,69,111,113,155,189,39,169,147,181,95,97,11,45,23,153,3,37,79,81,123,157,7,137,115,149,63,65,235,13,247,121,227,5,47,49,91,125,231,105,83,117,31,33,203,237,215,89,195,229,15,17,59,93,199,73,51,85,255]
+key = 0x47
+out = bytearray()
+for b in raw:
+    # encrypted = (alpha + v * inv8[key>>1]) mod 256
+    # So: v * inv8[key>>1] = (encrypted - alpha) mod 256
+    # v = (encrypted - alpha) * (2*(key>>1) + 1) mod 256  [since inv8 is modular inverse of odd]
+    diff = (b - 47) & 0xFF
+    odd = (2 * (key >> 1) + 1) & 0xFF
+    v = (diff * odd) & 0xFF
+    out.append(v)
+    key = (1 + 2 * v) & 0xFF
+open('Nab0013-original.bin', 'wb').write(out)
+print(f'Extracted {len(out)} bytes')
+"
+# Then flash Nab0013-original.bin via JTAG instead of Nab.bin
+```
+
+This produces the exact 105,128-byte original firmware. Use this as a last resort
+if the rebuilt WPA2/3 firmware doesn't boot.
+
+---
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+### "JTAG scan chain interrogation failed"
+- Check all 7 wires (especially GND)
+- Make sure the rabbit is powered on
+- Try lower speed: edit `rpi1-gpio.cfg`, change `adapter speed 100` to `50`
+- Verify J29 pin numbering matches schematic (pin 1 = square pad = 3.3V)
+
+### "Error: timed out while waiting for target halted"
+- The reset config may need tweaking. Try:
+  ```
+  reset_config trst_and_srst separate
+  ```
+- Or try holding the rabbit's reset manually while connecting
+
+### flash write_image fails
+- Verify file path is correct
+- Try erasing first: `flash erase_sector 0 0 last` then write without `erase` flag
+- Check that the `.bin` file is < 124KB (128KB - 4KB config sector)
+
+### Rabbit still shows random LEDs after flash
+- The bytecode in the firmware may still be bad
+- Try flashing the original `Nab0013-original.bin` instead (see Alternative section)
+- Use `verify_image` to confirm the flash contents match the file
+
+### OpenOCD crashes or GPIO errors
+- Must run as root (`sudo`)
+- Make sure no other process is using the GPIO pins
+- Verify `/dev/mem` is accessible
+
+---
+
